#!/usr/bin/env python3.11
r"""Is every LOADED launchd job's full argv pointing somewhere real, and
somewhere that is not the agent-platform working checkout? And is every
deploy-pinned area's installed commit still what main ships? (platform/decisions/0059)

WHY THIS EXISTS, SPECIFICALLY. #469's own completion measurement for the
deploy-area migration read only `ProgramArguments[0]` — the interpreter — and
called a job "migrated" once that one entry pointed at the deploy area. Five
jobs whose actual SCRIPT sits in argv[1] behind an interpreter (`python3
/path/to/script`, or `/bin/sh /path/to/script`) were counted as done while
argv[1] still pointed at `~/.local/bin/<name>`, a symlink straight into the
checkout (ARCH-229) — exactly the unattended-firing-reads-a-branch hazard
ADR-0059 exists to close. A checker that only reads argv[0] reproduces that
exact defect. This one walks EVERY argv entry, which is the whole point.

Separately: `com.convivy.waiting-on-operator-digest` has been LOADED and failing
(`launchctl` exit status 2) every Monday since 2026-07-28, because commit
168f6b1 deleted `bin/waiting-on-operator-digest` from git while its live plist
stayed loaded — its `~/.local/bin/waiting-on-operator-digest` symlink now resolves
to nothing. Nothing noticed for five weeks, because a job silently failing to
exec looks, from the outside, exactly like a job that ran and did nothing.

BEHAVIOUR. Enumerates every job `launchctl list` reports loaded, reads each
one's full argv via `launchctl print`, and reports two distinct breaches:

  (a) DANGLING     — an absolute argv entry whose resolved (`os.path.realpath`)
                      target does not exist. A loaded job that cannot exec is
                      dead and silent.
  (b) CHECKOUT-REACHING — an absolute argv entry that resolves inside
                      `<root>/agent-platform/`. Whatever branch that
                      checkout sits on is the code an unattended firing runs.

An argv entry following a shell interpreter's `-c` (`/bin/sh -c '<cmd>'`) is
read as a command string: every shell word in it that is absolute is checked
on its own, rather than realpath'ing the whole string with its flags and
operators, or trusting only its first word (a wrapper like `env`, `timeout`
or `nice` in front of the real program would otherwise hide it).

`com.convivy.agent-platform-autopull` is exempted BY LABEL from (b): it is the CD
watcher itself, so reaching into the checkout is how it learns what to
deploy, and it must never run FROM the deploy area it writes — pinning it
there would mean an update to its own logic could never take effect. Every
other job's checkout-reaching argv is the drift this file exists to catch;
this one job is the one case where reaching the checkout is correct.

SCOPE. Only labels this platform owns (`com.convivy.*` — every plist in
`launchd/` uses it, confirmed by `ls launchd/*.plist`), plus — temporarily,
through the CQ-428 label migration — any OTHER label a sweep finds loaded
whose suffix matches one of those plists' bare job name (e.g. a job still
loaded as `<pre-rename label>.run-observer` is still `run-observer`,
whatever its prefix). That second case is found by suffix match against
what `launchctl list` actually reports, never by hardcoding the pre-rename
label text (CQ-428's whole point is removing that text, not writing it
again here). macOS's own agents are excluded on purpose: several ship `ProgramArguments`
entries that resolve to nothing on disk at all (`CVMCompiler`,
`PosterBoard`) because they live only in the dyld shared cache / sealed
system volume, never as a standalone file — a real condition, unrelated to
this migration, that would swamp every run's findings in system noise if
this checked every loaded label rather than the ones this platform put there.

A pass is silent. A breach prints the label, the offending argv entry, and
its resolved target to stderr, and logs ONE alert signal per breach class present
this run (not one per job — see `main`), keyed so an unchanged breach set does
not log again daily. A breach that clears and returns within the same UTC day logs
nothing after that day's first signal, unless its key differs: the key carries the set of
label and argv pairs, so a return with a different set logs again. It never creates a work item (platform/decisions/0073). A `launchctl print` that fails for one label is skipped
with a note, not a crash. Exits non-zero when any breach was found.

A SECOND, INDEPENDENT CHECK: is a deploy-pinned AREA itself behind what it is
pinned to. `run-register-api` was found on 2026-09-12 serving a deploy
installed 2026-08-31, nine days after six merged commits to `bin/run-register-api`
— four of them fail-closed privacy fixes — went live nowhere, because that
area has its own installer and nothing refreshes it (`agent-platform-autopull`
only refreshes the shared deploy area). The argv check above cannot see this:
a dangling-or-checkout-reaching walk only asks whether a LOADED job's argv
resolves, and a job reading a real, existing file in a deploy area that is
simply stale passes it clean.

For every directory directly under the deploy root (default
`~/.local/deploy`, `--deploy-root` to override) holding a `DEPLOYED_COMMIT`
file, `classify_deploy_area` reads that file's `commit:` and
`installed_from:` lines, and in the repository named by `installed_from`
counts the commits on `origin/main` after `commit:` that touch a path the
area ships. The area's shipped paths are everything on disk under the area,
relative to the area's own root (no per-area list to go stale), excluding
`venv/` at the area's root and any `__pycache__` directory anywhere under
it — what the installer built, never something main ships, and excluding
it is not free of cost: measured on this machine, agent-platform-shared
carries 3,616 files on disk and only 196 outside those two, bosun-server
3,392 and 7, run-register-api 1,489 and 21, so every one of the rest would
otherwise be a dead pathspec entry on every `git rev-list` call this check
makes. A path that exists in the deploy area but not in the repository
still just never matches any commit, costing only a pathspec entry. One
match is enough to report the area STALE, naming the deployed commit and
how many commits it lags. An area whose lag cannot be computed —
`installed_from` missing from `DEPLOYED_COMMIT`, the named repository
absent or not a git checkout, the deployed commit unknown there,
`origin/main` unresolvable there, a directory under the area that could not
be listed, or an area with no shipped paths at all (every real installer
ships at least `bin/`, so an empty result only means something went
wrong) — is reported UNCHECKABLE, never silently treated as current: an
unreadable state must never read as a clean one, same rule
`check-primary-checkouts` follows for an unreadable project registry. Both
STALE and UNCHECKABLE areas go through the same per-breach-class signal,
day-scoped dedup key, and non-zero exit as the argv breaches above.

This check reads only local git refs (`rev-parse`, `cat-file`, `rev-list`)
against whatever `origin/main` the named repository already has — it never
runs `git fetch` and makes no network call, so a repository whose remote
tracking ref is itself stale reports against that stale ref rather than
reaching out to update it.

RE-RUN THIS BY HAND. The shell equivalent of the argv-walk:

    for label in $(launchctl list | awk -F'\t' 'NR>1 && $3!=""{print $3}'); do
      echo "== $label =="
      launchctl print "gui/$(id -u)/$label" | awk '/arguments = \{/{f=1;next} /^\t\}/{f=0} f'
    done

Each printed line under a label is one argv entry; `readlink -f` (or
`realpath`) any absolute one and check it exists and is not under
`<root>/agent-platform/`.

ARGV PATHS ARE NOT TREATED AS SECRET-BEARING. A breach report names the
offending argv entry verbatim in the signal it logs, because the path IS the
finding — a report saying only "some job reaches the checkout" cannot be acted
on. That is safe while a job's argv holds a program path and flags, which is all
any job here passes today. The signal lands on the alerts page, which more than
this process reads, so every line passes through `signal_client.sanitise_body`,
which redacts a credential-shaped value when it appears as written or with a
character it removes; a whitespace control or a kept format character inside
the prefix lets it through, a known gap not acted on. A job that encodes a
credential in a path component still should not do that, because sanitise
catches only the prefixes it knows.

SAFETY. Read-only. Runs `launchctl list` / `launchctl print` and, for the
deploy-area lag check, local `git` read commands (`rev-parse`, `cat-file`,
`rev-list`) against repositories already on disk; never loads, unloads, or
kickstarts anything, and never fetches, clones, or writes to a repository.
"""
from __future__ import annotations

import argparse
import datetime
import hashlib
import json
import os
import pathlib
import shlex
import subprocess
import sys
from typing import Any
sys.path.insert(0, str(pathlib.Path(__file__).resolve().parent.parent / "lib" / "agent-platform"))
import builder_root as BR  # noqa: E402

CHECKOUT_ROOT = str(BR.root() / "agent-platform")

# Every deploy-pinned area (install-run-register-api, install-agent-platform-shared,
# ...) writes its DEPLOYED_COMMIT one level under here. `pathlib.Path.home()`,
# not BR.root(): this is the account's own home, same convention
# check-primary-checkouts' LAUNCH_AGENTS uses, and is unrelated to the
# platform's configured CREWS_ROOT.
DEPLOY_ROOT = pathlib.Path.home() / ".local" / "deploy"

# Every plist this platform owns is com.convivy.* (`ls launchd/*.plist`) —
# see module docstring's SCOPE section for why everything else (macOS's own
# agents) is excluded rather than walked too.
OWNED_LABEL_PREFIXES = ("com.convivy.",)

# The CD watcher must read the checkout (that is how it learns what to
# deploy) and must never itself run from the deploy area it writes.
EXEMPT_LABELS = {"com.convivy.agent-platform-autopull"}

_LIB_DIR = pathlib.Path(__file__).resolve().parent.parent / "lib" / "agent-platform"


def _ensure_lib_on_path() -> None:
    """Put the installed platform lib on sys.path exactly once.

    Resolved relative to this file, not ~/.local/lib/agent-platform, for the
    same reason check-primary-checkouts does this: that farm symlinks into
    whichever branch the agent-platform checkout is on, which is exactly the
    hazard a deploy-pinned copy of this script must not reach back into.
    """
    entry = str(_LIB_DIR)
    if entry not in sys.path:
        sys.path.insert(0, entry)


_report_failures: int = 0


def _utc_today() -> str:
    """Today's UTC date, ISO format: the day scope of this monitor's dedup_key.

    A named seam so a test can pin the day. main() reads it once per run and
    passes that one value to every key the run writes."""
    return datetime.datetime.now(datetime.timezone.utc).date().isoformat()


def _names_digest(names: list[str]) -> str:
    """The identity of a set of names in a dedup_key. Joining them with `_` let `["x_y"]` and
    `["x", "y"]` produce one key, so the second distinct breach answered `duplicate`. A hash of the
    JSON list cannot collide that way (review of PR #788, rounds 1 and 2)."""
    return hashlib.sha256(json.dumps(list(names)).encode("utf-8")).hexdigest()[:16]


def _post_signal(product: str, title: str, body: str, dedup_key: str,
                 *, dry_run: bool = False, severity: str = "high") -> dict[str, Any]:
    """Log an alert signal for a breach. Best-effort. A signal, never a work item.

    The title leads the body, since a signal has no title field."""
    global _report_failures
    if dry_run:
        return {"status": "dry-run"}
    try:
        _ensure_lib_on_path()
        import signal_client  # noqa: E402
        status = signal_client.post_alert(
            "deploy-pinned-jobs", signal_client.sanitise_body(f"{title}\n\n{body}"),
            product=product, dedup_key=dedup_key, meta={"severity": severity})
        return {"status": status}
    except Exception as exc:
        _report_failures += 1
        print(f"[REPORT-ERROR] {product}: {exc}", file=sys.stderr)
        return {"status": f"error:{type(exc).__name__}"}


def parse_labels(list_output: str) -> list[str]:
    """`launchctl list` output -> loaded job labels.

    Tab-separated `PID\tStatus\tLabel`. Skips the header row and any row
    whose label column is empty after stripping.
    """
    labels: list[str] = []
    for line in list_output.splitlines():
        parts = line.split("\t")
        if len(parts) < 3:
            continue
        label = parts[-1].strip()
        if not label or label == "Label":
            continue
        labels.append(label)
    return labels


def parse_arguments_block(print_output: str) -> list[str]:
    """`launchctl print` transcript -> every entry of its `arguments = { }`
    block, in order. Every entry, not just the first — see module docstring."""
    args: list[str] = []
    in_block = False
    for line in print_output.splitlines():
        stripped = line.strip()
        if not in_block:
            if stripped == "arguments = {":
                in_block = True
            continue
        if stripped == "}":
            break
        if stripped:
            args.append(stripped)
    return args


_SHELL_BASENAMES = {"sh", "bash", "zsh"}


def _shell_command_targets(command: str) -> list[str]:
    """A `-c` command string's absolute tokens, in the order they appear.

    Tokenized with `shlex` (posix quoting, `punctuation_chars` on) so a
    redirection (`2>/dev/null`) or operator (`||`, `&&`) splits off as its
    own token instead of fusing with the path beside it — a wrapper
    (`env`, `timeout`, `nice`, ...) in front of the real program is just
    another token this same walk reaches, with no special-casing needed.
    A string `shlex` can't parse (unbalanced quotes) is still checked,
    split on whitespace instead of skipped.

    A token counts only when the token itself begins with "/". An absolute
    path fused to a prefix inside one token (`PYTHONPATH=/...`,
    `--opt=/...`) is not unwrapped and so is not checked — no loaded job
    passes a `-c` string shaped that way today.
    """
    try:
        lexer = shlex.shlex(command, posix=True, punctuation_chars=True)
        lexer.whitespace_split = True
        words = list(lexer)
    except ValueError:
        words = command.split()
    return [w for w in words if w.startswith("/")]


def classify(label: str, argv: list[str], *,
             checkout_root: str = CHECKOUT_ROOT) -> list[dict[str, str]]:
    """One job's argv -> its breach dicts (kind/label/argv/resolved).

    A dangling entry is reported as dangling only — resolving whether a
    nonexistent target's path STRING happens to fall under the checkout adds
    a claim ("reaching the checkout") a target that isn't there can't
    actually make good on.

    An entry that follows a shell interpreter's `-c` (`/bin/sh -c '<cmd>'`,
    matched by the preceding entry's basename in `_SHELL_BASENAMES`) is
    recognised as a command string before the absolute-path gate below, so
    it is tokenised and its tokens checked whatever the string's own first
    character is (`cd /x && ...`, `exec /...`). Every other entry still
    needs to start with "/" itself to be looked at. See
    `_shell_command_targets` for exactly which tokens of a command string
    are checked.
    """
    breaches: list[dict[str, str]] = []
    checkout_real = os.path.realpath(checkout_root)
    for i, entry in enumerate(argv):
        is_shell_command = (
            i >= 2 and argv[i - 1] == "-c"
            and os.path.basename(argv[i - 2]) in _SHELL_BASENAMES
        )
        if not is_shell_command and not entry.startswith("/"):
            continue
        targets = _shell_command_targets(entry) if is_shell_command else [entry]
        for target in targets:
            resolved = os.path.realpath(target)
            if not os.path.exists(resolved):
                breaches.append({"kind": "dangling", "label": label,
                                 "argv": entry, "resolved": resolved})
                continue
            if label in EXEMPT_LABELS:
                continue
            if resolved == checkout_real or resolved.startswith(checkout_real + os.sep):
                breaches.append({"kind": "checkout-reaching", "label": label,
                                 "argv": entry, "resolved": resolved})
    return breaches


def _parse_deployed_commit(text: str) -> dict[str, str]:
    """A DEPLOYED_COMMIT file's `key: value` lines -> a dict.

    A line with no colon, or a blank line, is skipped rather than raising —
    a hand-edited or partially-written file must read as a missing field,
    never crash the check that exists to notice it.
    """
    fields: dict[str, str] = {}
    for line in text.splitlines():
        line = line.strip()
        if not line or ":" not in line:
            continue
        key, _, value = line.partition(":")
        fields[key.strip()] = value.strip()
    return fields


def _deploy_areas(deploy_root: pathlib.Path) -> list[pathlib.Path]:
    """Every directory directly under `deploy_root` holding a DEPLOYED_COMMIT
    file, sorted by name for a stable run order. A missing deploy root reads
    as "nothing to check", same as a missing LAUNCH_AGENTS dir elsewhere."""
    try:
        candidates = sorted(deploy_root.iterdir())
    except OSError:
        return []
    return [d for d in candidates if d.is_dir() and (d / "DEPLOYED_COMMIT").is_file()]


class _ShippedPathsUnreadable(Exception):
    """`_shipped_paths` could not list every directory under the area."""


def _shipped_paths(area: pathlib.Path) -> list[str]:
    """Every file under `area`, relative to `area` itself, as POSIX strings —
    derived from what the area actually holds on disk rather than a
    per-area list that would go stale the moment an installer's file set
    changes. DEPLOYED_COMMIT itself is excluded: it is this check's own
    marker, never a file main ships. `venv/` directly under `area` and any
    `__pycache__` directory at any depth are excluded too: what the
    installer built, never something main ships — see the module docstring
    for the per-area file counts that exclusion is worth.

    Raises `_ShippedPathsUnreadable` if any directory under `area` could not
    be listed, rather than silently returning whatever the walk reached
    before the failure: a short, incomplete result must never be mistaken
    for a complete one.
    """
    errors: list[OSError] = []
    paths: list[str] = []
    for root, dirs, files in os.walk(area, onerror=errors.append):
        root_path = pathlib.Path(root)
        if root_path == area and "venv" in dirs:
            dirs.remove("venv")
        if "__pycache__" in dirs:
            dirs.remove("__pycache__")
        for name in files:
            rel = (root_path / name).relative_to(area)
            if rel == pathlib.Path("DEPLOYED_COMMIT"):
                continue
            paths.append(rel.as_posix())
    if errors:
        raise _ShippedPathsUnreadable(
            f"could not list {len(errors)} director{'y' if len(errors) == 1 else 'ies'} "
            f"under {area}: {errors[0]}")
    return sorted(paths)


def _run_git(repo: pathlib.Path, *args: str) -> subprocess.CompletedProcess | None:
    """One read-only git call against `repo`, or None if it could not even
    run (missing repo dir, no git on PATH, a timeout) — never raises."""
    try:
        return subprocess.run(["git", "-C", str(repo), *args],
                              capture_output=True, text=True, timeout=30)
    except (OSError, subprocess.SubprocessError):
        return None


def classify_deploy_area(area: pathlib.Path) -> dict[str, Any]:
    """One deploy area -> its status dict: `area` (name) and `status`
    (`"current"`, `"stale"`, or `"uncheckable"`), plus the fields that status
    carries (`deployed_commit`/`lag` for stale, `reason` for uncheckable).

    Every early return below is "uncheckable", never "current" — an
    unreadable `DEPLOYED_COMMIT`, a missing `installed_from`, a repository
    that is gone or is not a git checkout, a deployed commit unknown there,
    an unresolvable `origin/main`, a directory under the area that could
    not be listed, or an area with no shipped paths at all (every real
    installer ships at least `bin/`, so an empty result only means
    something went wrong) must never read as "nothing to report" (the same
    rule `check-primary-checkouts` follows for an unreadable project
    registry).
    """
    name = area.name
    try:
        fields = _parse_deployed_commit((area / "DEPLOYED_COMMIT").read_text(encoding="utf-8"))
    except OSError as exc:
        return {"area": name, "status": "uncheckable",
                "reason": f"could not read DEPLOYED_COMMIT: {exc}"}

    commit = fields.get("commit")
    if not commit:
        return {"area": name, "status": "uncheckable",
                "reason": "DEPLOYED_COMMIT has no commit: line"}

    installed_from = fields.get("installed_from")
    if not installed_from:
        return {"area": name, "status": "uncheckable", "deployed_commit": commit,
                "reason": "DEPLOYED_COMMIT has no installed_from: line"}

    repo = pathlib.Path(installed_from)
    proc = _run_git(repo, "rev-parse", "--git-dir")
    if proc is None or proc.returncode != 0:
        return {"area": name, "status": "uncheckable", "deployed_commit": commit,
                "reason": f"installed_from {installed_from} is not a git repository"}

    proc = _run_git(repo, "cat-file", "-e", f"{commit}^{{commit}}")
    if proc is None or proc.returncode != 0:
        return {"area": name, "status": "uncheckable", "deployed_commit": commit,
                "reason": f"commit {commit} is unknown in {installed_from}"}

    proc = _run_git(repo, "rev-parse", "--verify", "origin/main")
    if proc is None or proc.returncode != 0:
        return {"area": name, "status": "uncheckable", "deployed_commit": commit,
                "reason": f"origin/main does not resolve in {installed_from}"}

    try:
        shipped = _shipped_paths(area)
    except _ShippedPathsUnreadable as exc:
        return {"area": name, "status": "uncheckable", "deployed_commit": commit,
                "reason": str(exc)}
    if not shipped:
        return {"area": name, "status": "uncheckable", "deployed_commit": commit,
                "reason": f"{area} has no shipped paths to check"}

    proc = _run_git(repo, "rev-list", "--count", f"{commit}..origin/main", "--", *shipped)
    if proc is None or proc.returncode != 0:
        return {"area": name, "status": "uncheckable", "deployed_commit": commit,
                "reason": f"git rev-list could not count commits in {installed_from}"}

    lag = int(proc.stdout.strip() or "0")
    if lag == 0:
        return {"area": name, "status": "current", "deployed_commit": commit, "lag": 0}
    return {"area": name, "status": "stale", "deployed_commit": commit, "lag": lag,
            "installed_from": installed_from}


def _launchctl_list() -> str:
    proc = subprocess.run(["launchctl", "list"], capture_output=True,
                          text=True, timeout=30)
    return proc.stdout


def _migrating_label_suffixes(checkout_root: str) -> tuple[str, ...]:
    """`.{job}` for every bare job name this platform's launchd/ ships as
    com.convivy.{job}.plist — used to recognize that SAME job still loaded
    under a pre-CQ-428 label, by suffix match against what `launchctl list`
    reports, rather than by this file ever naming that label itself.
    """
    launchd_dir = pathlib.Path(checkout_root) / "launchd"
    if not launchd_dir.is_dir():
        return ()
    prefix = "com.convivy."
    return tuple(f".{p.stem[len(prefix):]}" for p in launchd_dir.glob(f"{prefix}*.plist"))


def _launchctl_print(label: str, uid: int) -> tuple[str, int]:
    proc = subprocess.run(["launchctl", "print", f"gui/{uid}/{label}"],
                          capture_output=True, text=True, timeout=30)
    return proc.stdout, proc.returncode


_DANGLING_LEAD = (
    "A scheduled job on this machine is loaded and will fire on its own "
    "schedule, but the program it is set to run is not there anymore, so "
    "every firing fails to start anything. Nothing about that failure is "
    "loud: an exec that can't find its target looks, from the outside, "
    "exactly like a job that ran and quietly did nothing."
)

_CHECKOUT_LEAD = (
    "A scheduled job on this machine is reading its program straight out of "
    "the agent-platform working checkout instead of the pinned copy built "
    "for it, so whatever branch that checkout happens to be on right now is "
    "the code it runs unattended."
)


def _body(lead: str, breaches: list[dict[str, str]]) -> str:
    lines = [lead, ""]
    for b in sorted(breaches, key=lambda b: (b["label"], b["argv"])):
        lines.append(f"- `{b['label']}`: `{b['argv']}` -> `{b['resolved']}`")
    return "\n".join(lines)


_STALE_DEPLOY_LEAD = (
    "A deploy-pinned area's DEPLOYED_COMMIT is behind commits already merged "
    "to main that touch a file the area ships, so a merged, reviewed change "
    "is not live there until someone reinstalls it."
)

_UNCHECKABLE_DEPLOY_LEAD = (
    "A deploy-pinned area's lag behind main could not be computed, so "
    "whether a merged change has reached it is unknown rather than "
    "confirmed current."
)


def _stale_deploy_body(breaches: list[dict[str, Any]]) -> str:
    lines = [_STALE_DEPLOY_LEAD, ""]
    for b in sorted(breaches, key=lambda b: b["area"]):
        plural = "" if b["lag"] == 1 else "s"
        lines.append(f"- `{b['area']}`: deployed `{b['deployed_commit']}`, "
                      f"{b['lag']} commit{plural} behind origin/main")
    return "\n".join(lines)


def _uncheckable_deploy_body(breaches: list[dict[str, Any]]) -> str:
    lines = [_UNCHECKABLE_DEPLOY_LEAD, ""]
    for b in sorted(breaches, key=lambda b: b["area"]):
        lines.append(f"- `{b['area']}`: {b['reason']}")
    return "\n".join(lines)


def main(argv: list[str] | None = None) -> int:
    parser = argparse.ArgumentParser(prog="check-deploy-pinned-jobs",
                                     description=__doc__)
    parser.add_argument("--uid", type=int, default=os.getuid() if hasattr(os, "getuid") else 0,
                        help="uid launchctl's gui/<uid>/<label> domain uses (for testing)")
    parser.add_argument("--checkout-root", default=CHECKOUT_ROOT,
                        help="override the agent-platform checkout root (for testing)")
    parser.add_argument("--deploy-root", default=str(DEPLOY_ROOT),
                        help="override the deploy areas root (for testing)")
    parser.add_argument("--no-report", action="store_true",
                        help="detect breaches but do not log signals (dry run)")
    parser.add_argument("--verbose", "-v", action="store_true",
                        help="print per-job details even when passing")
    opts = parser.parse_args(argv)

    global _report_failures
    _report_failures = 0

    migrating_suffixes = _migrating_label_suffixes(opts.checkout_root)
    labels = [label for label in parse_labels(_launchctl_list())
              if label.startswith(OWNED_LABEL_PREFIXES)
              or label.endswith(migrating_suffixes)]

    dangling: list[dict[str, str]] = []
    checkout_reaching: list[dict[str, str]] = []

    for label in labels:
        text, rc = _launchctl_print(label, opts.uid)
        if rc != 0:
            if opts.verbose:
                print(f"[SKIP] {label}: launchctl print failed (exit {rc})",
                      file=sys.stderr)
            continue
        job_argv = parse_arguments_block(text)
        for b in classify(label, job_argv, checkout_root=opts.checkout_root):
            print(f"[BREACH] {b['kind']} {b['label']}: {b['argv']} -> "
                  f"{b['resolved']}", file=sys.stderr)
            (dangling if b["kind"] == "dangling" else checkout_reaching).append(b)
        if opts.verbose and not job_argv:
            print(f"[OK] {label}: no argv parsed", file=sys.stderr)

    stale_deploy: list[dict[str, Any]] = []
    uncheckable_deploy: list[dict[str, Any]] = []
    for area in _deploy_areas(pathlib.Path(opts.deploy_root)):
        result = classify_deploy_area(area)
        if result["status"] == "stale":
            print(f"[BREACH] stale-deploy {result['area']}: deployed "
                  f"{result['deployed_commit']} is {result['lag']} commit(s) "
                  "behind origin/main", file=sys.stderr)
            stale_deploy.append(result)
        elif result["status"] == "uncheckable":
            print(f"[BREACH] uncheckable-deploy {result['area']}: {result['reason']}",
                  file=sys.stderr)
            uncheckable_deploy.append(result)
        elif opts.verbose:
            print(f"[OK] {result['area']}: at origin/main", file=sys.stderr)

    if not dangling and not checkout_reaching and not stale_deploy and not uncheckable_deploy:
        if opts.verbose:
            print("[OK] every loaded job's argv resolves and stays out of "
                  "the checkout, and every deploy area is at origin/main",
                  file=sys.stderr)
        return 0

    day = _utc_today()
    if dangling:
        ids = sorted(f"{b['label']}:{b['argv']}" for b in dangling)
        title = f"[deploy-pinned-jobs] {len(dangling)} loaded argv entr{'y' if len(dangling)==1 else 'ies'} point nowhere"
        res = _post_signal("agent-platform", title, _body(_DANGLING_LEAD, dangling),
                           dedup_key=f"deploy-pinned-jobs:dangling:{_names_digest(ids)}:{day}",
                        dry_run=opts.no_report)
        print(f"[DANGLING] {len(dangling)} → {res.get('status', '?')}", file=sys.stderr)

    if checkout_reaching:
        ids = sorted(f"{b['label']}:{b['argv']}" for b in checkout_reaching)
        title = (f"[deploy-pinned-jobs] {len(checkout_reaching)} loaded job(s) "
                f"reading the agent-platform checkout")
        res = _post_signal("agent-platform", title, _body(_CHECKOUT_LEAD, checkout_reaching),
                           dedup_key=f"deploy-pinned-jobs:checkout-reaching:{_names_digest(ids)}:{day}",
                        dry_run=opts.no_report)
        print(f"[CHECKOUT-REACHING] {len(checkout_reaching)} → {res.get('status', '?')}",
              file=sys.stderr)

    if stale_deploy:
        ids = sorted(f"{b['area']}:{b['deployed_commit']}" for b in stale_deploy)
        n = len(stale_deploy)
        title = f"[deploy-pinned-jobs] {n} deploy area{'' if n == 1 else 's'} behind main"
        res = _post_signal("agent-platform", title, _stale_deploy_body(stale_deploy),
                           dedup_key=f"deploy-pinned-jobs:stale-deploy:{_names_digest(ids)}:{day}",
                        dry_run=opts.no_report)
        print(f"[STALE-DEPLOY] {n} → {res.get('status', '?')}", file=sys.stderr)

    if uncheckable_deploy:
        ids = sorted(f"{b['area']}:{b['reason']}" for b in uncheckable_deploy)
        n = len(uncheckable_deploy)
        title = (f"[deploy-pinned-jobs] {n} deploy area{'' if n == 1 else 's'} "
                f"could not be checked for lag")
        res = _post_signal(
            "agent-platform", title, _uncheckable_deploy_body(uncheckable_deploy),
            dedup_key=f"deploy-pinned-jobs:uncheckable-deploy:{_names_digest(ids)}:{day}",
            dry_run=opts.no_report)
        print(f"[UNCHECKABLE-DEPLOY] {n} → {res.get('status', '?')}", file=sys.stderr)

    if _report_failures > 0:
        print(f"[REPORT-FAIL] {_report_failures} report(s) failed — "
              "breaches not all logged", file=sys.stderr)
        return 3
    return 2


if __name__ == "__main__":
    raise SystemExit(main())
